    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "In short, **no, you should not make anything of the fact that the selection panel was made up entirely of women.** It is very unlikely to be a sign of bias or an agenda against you.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why, along with what is more likely driving the outcome:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Composition Does Not Indicate Bias\n* **Gender is neutral here:** Both you and the successful candidate are women. An all-female panel choosing one female candidate over another does not suggest gender-based discrimination or favoritism against you.\n* **Organizational demographics:** At senior levels in banking (e.g., Risk, Compliance, HR, Legal), panels often reflect the specific stakeholders involved (such as the Chief Risk Officer, Head of Compliance, Head of Audit, and an HR Business Partner). Having four women on a panel is simply reflective of who held those leadership seats.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strategic Fit vs. Technical Depth\nFor a **Head of Risk Modelling** role, the requirements often diverge significantly between hands-on quantitative expertise and executive governance:\n\n* **Your profile:** A PhD in Statistics and nine years of direct modelling experience represents strong, technical quantitative depth.\n* **The successful candidate’s profile:** A Master's in Finance paired with \"broad experience in regulatory affairs\" suggests a strong background in regulatory navigation (e.g., Basel III/IV, SR 11-7, PRA/ECB compliance, stress testing governance).\n* **Leadership priorities:** Banks frequently prioritize regulatory negotiation, executive communication, and model risk governance over pure statistical depth when hiring at the *Head of* level. The panel likely determined that regulatory affairs and stakeholder management were the primary gaps or priorities for the department.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Lack of Feedback (The 2-Week Silence)\n* **Standard corporate delays:** Two weeks without written feedback after a senior-level search is very common. Written feedback usually requires HR coordination and sometimes legal review to avoid liability.\n* **Not necessarily malicious:** Hiring teams often move on immediately to onboarding the candidate, and non-automated written feedback tends to be deprioritized unless followed up on.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up politely on feedback:** Send a brief, constructive note to the recruiter or hiring lead:\n   > *\"Thank you again for the opportunity to interview for the Head of Risk Modelling role. I understand the team has made its final decision. I would greatly appreciate any brief feedback or guidance you can share to help me understand where I can develop for similar leadership opportunities in the future.\"*\n2. **Focus inquiries on competencies, not panel demographics:** Any mention or questioning of the panel's gender composition will likely come across as defensive and could damage valuable professional relationships in a small, specialized industry.\n3. **Target your positioning:** If you want a \"Head of\" role, evaluate how to position your statistical expertise alongside regulatory strategy, audit defense, and executive leadership.\n",
